  "body": "## Result\n\nAn exact, source-bound counterexample to transporting ideal affine-lattice closure through an admitted internally rounded leaf. This is **not a bug in PR628 or PR1848**, and does not change the selected supplier or kappa.\n\nFor the pinned PR628 length-one outside leaf (`b=1/4`, phase `(3/5,4/5)`, beta `1/2`, working precision 4), source `(-17,4)` and zero target produce **`(39/8,-1/16)`**, whereas the exact ideal operator produces `(5,0)`. The ideal shear preserves the all-integer lattice `x=(-17+4i)k, y=5j`; the actual admitted implementation does not. A later exact-addition pair using the **unchanged** actual real output with precisions 0 and 4 leaves `1/8`. Same-grid pairs and the leaf's own literal inverse restore exactly.\n\nThe README gives the independent rational trace and a sharp half-open rounding-cell admission repair. Higher execution precision provides a positive example where the later pair cancels even though exact lattice closure still fails.\n\n## Scope and predecessors\n\n- Base: `0b1cccf86e2f5132ed286a26ef0a681a495740ea`.\n- PR628 source head: `0768f97d06c623b8211e651543c66135796b62bf`, retained through `dirty-prefix-precision`; every imported source is Git-blob/SHA256 pinned.\n- #1848 (`d975e19e7e931fc2b45792a4f7140ac13759a7da`) already proves the conditional affine-lattice criterion. This addresses its **explicit open question about actual rounded-source closure**, not a false statement attributed to its theorem.\n- #1853 (`43caf3d864f4328a436bda40f849d96e2bf209cb`) covers bounded scalar floor sums. Neither existing criterion is claimed new here.\n- Targeted live checks reconfirmed both heads. Cached overlap coverage/limitations are recorded; no novelty certificate is claimed.\n- Coordination: https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1884 . Assigned card: `uniform-transfer-audit`, instance 09.\n\nOnly `research/rounded-leaf-lattice-closure/` is added. Frozen sources, selected record, shared indexes and workflows remain unchanged.\n\n## Verification actually run\n\nmacOS arm64, CPython **3.12.12**, standard library only:\n\n```sh\nuv run --python 3.12 --no-project python scripts/research_package.py --package research/rounded-leaf-lattice-closure\nuv run --python 3.12 --no-project python -I -O -B research/rounded-leaf-lattice-closure/verify.py --witness-only\n```\n\nObserved `PASS_REGISTERED_RESEARCH_PACKAGE` and `PASS_ROUNDED_LEAF_LATTICE_CLOSURE_WITNESS`; combined final command duration 1.22 seconds.\n\nThe registered replay checks **8,064 actual leaf cases and 8,064 literal inverses**, 4,032 exact Gaussian-unit positive controls, 3,456 increment lattice-escape cases, and 3,600 sharp-cell checks. Other controls cover dirty targets, signed ties, matched precision, higher precision without exact lattice closure, input pin corruption, and rejected underprecision/off-grid/nonunit/aliased calls. No floating point, full repository suite or full selected cold replay was used.\n\n## Limits and next question\n\nThis is an admitted generic primitive input, not a proved occurrence in a selected multiplier or a particular prepared prime-power kernel. Finite census evidence is not a universal compiler proof. The all-integer ideal closure and safe-cell statements have written proofs; one exact instance falsifies the proposed shortcut.\n\nNext: for a concrete caller, propagate a source-dependent error enclosure through the actual rounded updates and certify containment in every later mixed-precision equality cell, including dirt and complete precision cost. No such selected-caller policy or cost saving is claimed here.\n\nCredit Chafik Boukhalfa/chafreaky, Gareth Davidson/bitplane, woklerx, and the analytic lineage retained in PR628's NOTICE. Prepared for adamgall with substantial **OpenAI Codex** assistance in derivation, implementation and prose; Apache-2.0. No independent human review or literature-priority claim.\n",
